{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>For this Moshe — the man.</b> As its Targum renders — a term of delay, and likewise ״בֹּשֵׁשׁ רִכְבּוֹ״ (Judges 5), ״וַיָּחִילוּ עַד בוֹשׁ״ (ibid. 3). For when Moshe ascended the mountain he said to them: at the end of forty days I am coming within six hours. They thought that the day he ascended was counted in the number, but he told them full ones — forty days and its night with it — and the day of his ascent has no night with it, for he ascended on the 7th of Sivan; thus the fortieth day is the 17th of Tammuz. On the 16th the Adversary came and threw the world into confusion, and showed the likeness of darkness and gloom and confusion, to say: surely Moshe has died — therefore confusion has come to the world. He said to them: Moshe has died, for six hours have already passed and he has not come, etc., as is in tractate Shabbat (89a). And it is impossible to say that they erred only on the counted day, whether before noon or after noon — for Moshe did not descend until the morrow, as it says ״וַיַּשְׁכִּימוּ מִמָּחֳרָת וַיַּעֲלוּ עֹלֹת״." "<b>That shall go before us.</b> They wanted many gods for themselves (Sanhedrin 63a)." "<b>For this Moshe — the man.</b> The Adversary showed them something like the likeness of Moshe, carried in the air of the firmament of the heavens (Shabbat 89a)." "<b>Who brought us up from the land of Egypt.</b> And he would show us the way by which we ascended; now we need gods that shall go before us."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:56:06.755741141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}